  "art.7.author": {"en": "Atlantic Notebook", "gl": "Caderno Atlántico"},
  "art.7.category": {"en": "Guides & Surroundings", "gl": "Guías e contorna"},
  "art.7.content": {"en": "In Aguiño, three days are measured not by a succession of visits, but by the breathing of the estuary. Dawn belongs to the fish market, midday to the harbour and the evening to the street, when the light falls over Sálvora and the village recovers its seafaring cadence. From the penthouse, by the harbour and facing the island, the stay allows you to follow that rhythm without turning it into an itinerary: simply let the tide arrange the hours.\n\n### I. Day 1. The fish market at dawn and the harbour in half-light\n\nThe first morning is best begun very early at Aguiño fish market. On weekdays, an auction is held before dawn, although times and access conditions may vary; the sensible course is to confirm them at the time and arrive with time to spare. It is not merely a matter of watching the catch come ashore, but of understanding where the cuisine of the estuary begins: the movement of the boats, the voices of those at work and the quiet precision of an activity that still sets the local pulse.\n\nAfterwards, the harbour invites an unhurried pause. A walk among boats, warehouses and quays reveals the true scale of Aguiño, while the local taverns provide a natural setting in which to discover the day’s shellfish and catch. Availability depends on the season and the fish market, as befits a cuisine governed by the tides rather than the calendar.\n\nThe afternoon can be set aside for a walk through the streets and a return to the penthouse before sunset. The terrace then becomes a private lookout over Sálvora: a sunset without the need to go anywhere, as the estuary slowly falls silent.\n\n### II. Day 2. Dunes, lighthouse and the Barbanza coast\n\nThe second day opens out towards Corrubedo Natural Park, approximately half an hour by car from Aguiño. Its dunes, coastal landscape and lighthouse surroundings offer a different reading of the shore: here the ocean seems less bound to harbour life and more exposed to the elements, the wind and the sand.\n\nIt is worth devoting the morning and part of the afternoon to the park, without forcing the day into a fixed route. The visit depends on the day’s conditions, the access points that are open and the time available, so checking the latest information before setting out is advisable. The return journey can follow the Barbanza coast, allowing the drive to become part of the day rather than a mere link between two points.\n\nBack in Aguiño, the night settles into a different pace. The estuary is no longer contemplated as a landscape, but felt as a presence: a dark line beyond the windows, the distant murmur of the harbour and the certainty that the following day will begin before the clock does.\n\n### III. Day 3. Sálvora, mussel rafts and the night of shellfishing\n\nThe third day can be devoted to the island of Sálvora, with boats departing from Aguiño during the season. Places are limited and the visit requires the corresponding park authorisation, so availability, boarding conditions and the marine forecast should be checked in advance. If the sea prevents departure, the alternative is not a renunciation, but another way of remaining close to the water: following the shoreline, observing the mussel rafts and drawing closer to the world of mussel farming from the coast and the estuary’s harbours.\n\nAs a final note, at low tide during the day, the shellfish gatherers can be seen working the beds from the harbour promenade: a direct way to understand the land, without setting foot on the sand.\n\nThe difference between visiting Aguiño and sleeping in Aguiño lies in that continuity: the estuary at dawn, the silence of the night and the fish market ten metres away. The penthouse is not a refuge set apart from the village, but its serene observatory, open to the rhythms that make each day a different voyage.", "gl": "En Aguiño, tres días non se miden pola sucesión de visitas, senón pola respiración da ría. O amencer pertence á lonxa, o mediodía ao porto e a tarde á rúa, cando a luz cae sobre Sálvora e a vila recupera a súa cadencia mariñeira. Desde o ático, no porto e fronte á illa, a estadía permite seguir ese compás sen convertelo nunha axenda: abonda con deixar que a marea ordene as horas.\n\n### I. Día 1. A lonxa ao amencer e o porto a media luz\n\nA primeira mañá convén comezala ben cedo na lonxa de Aguiño. Os días laborables celébrase unha poxa de madrugada, aínda que os horarios e as condicións de acceso poden variar; o máis sensato é confirmalos no momento e acudir con tempo de sobra. Non se trata só de observar a chegada do produto do mar, senón de comprender onde nace a cociña da ría: o movemento das embarcacións, as voces de quen traballa e a precisión silenciosa dunha actividade que aínda marca o pulso local.\n\nDespois, o porto pide unha pausa sen itinerario. O paseo entre embarcacións, almacéns e peiraos permite recoñecer a escala real de Aguiño, mentres as tabernas da zona ofrecen un lugar natural para achegarse ao marisco e ao peixe da xornada. A dispoñibilidade depende da tempada e da lonxa, como corresponde a unha cociña gobernada polas mareas e non polo calendario.\n\nA tarde pode reservarse para camiñar pola rúa e regresar ao ático antes do solpor. A terraza convértese daquela nunha atalaia doméstica sobre Sálvora: un solpor sen desprazamentos, coa ría entrando amodo no silencio.\n\n### II. Día 2. Dunas, faro e costa do Barbanza\n\nO segundo día ábrese cara ao Parque Natural de Corrubedo, situado aproximadamente a media hora en coche desde Aguiño. As súas dunas, a súa paisaxe litoral e os arredores do faro ofrecen unha lectura distinta da costa: aquí o océano aparece menos vencellado ao traballo portuario e máis exposto á intemperie, ao vento e á area.\n\nConvén dedicar a mañá e parte da tarde ao parque, sen forzar unha ruta pechada. A visita depende das condicións do día, dos accesos habilitados e do tempo dispoñible, polo que é recomendable consultar a información vixente antes de saír. O regreso pode facerse pola costa do Barbanza, deixando que o traxecto forme parte da xornada e non sexa unha simple ligazón entre dous puntos.\n\nDe volta en Aguiño, a noite recupera outro ritmo. A ría xa non se contempla como paisaxe, senón como presenza: unha liña escura detrás dos cristais, o rumor afastado do porto e a certeza de que o día seguinte volverá comezar antes ca o reloxo.\n\n### III. Día 3. Sálvora, bateas e a noite do marisqueo\n\nA terceira xornada pode dedicarse á illa de Sálvora, con barcos desde Aguiño durante a tempada. As prazas son limitadas e a visita require a correspondente autorización do parque, polo que convén comprobar con antelación a dispoñibilidade, as condicións de embarque e a previsión marítima. Se o mar impide a saída, a alternativa non é unha renuncia, senón outra maneira de permanecer canda a auga: percorrer o litoral, observar as bateas e achegarse ao mundo do mexillón desde a costa e os portos da ría.\n\nComo remate, nunha baixamar diúrna pódese ver as mariscadoras traballaren nos bancos desde o paseo do porto: unha maneira directa de comprender o territorio, sen pisar a area.\n\nA diferenza entre visitar Aguiño e durmir en Aguiño está nesa continuidade: a ría ao amencer, o silencio da noite e a lonxa a dez metros. O ático non funciona como refuxio á marxe da vila, senón como o seu observatorio sereno, aberto aos ritmos que fan de cada día unha navegación distinta."},
